      "text": "# Round 2 Independent Review: TypeScript CLI & Local Web-UI Foundation\n\n**Reviewer stance:** I have NOT seen the implementing agent's reasoning. I am evaluating the artefact cold, as if greenlighting it in a design review. I will cite specific lines, disagree where warranted, and identify the single call I'd push back hardest on.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Agreement with Prior Reviewers (Which Points Hold)\n\n### 1.1 Reviewer A's Phase 5 Rollback Critique (their Section 2.1)\n\n**Agree strongly.** Lines 454-466 describe a one-way door:\n\n```json\n\"bin\": { \"agent-config\": \"dist/cli/agent-config.js\" }\n```\n\nThe council TODO at lines 555-557 mandates shadow-mode, but Phase 5's acceptance gates (lines 504-511) make **zero mention** of observability, telemetry, or a feature flag to force the Bash path post-flip.\n\n**Additional evidence Reviewer A missed:** The Bash shim at lines 465-472 only works when `dist/cli/agent-config.js` is **missing** (dev-only fallback). Once the package ships, consumers have no escape hatch if the TS binary has a runtime panic. The `AGENT_CONFIG_LEGACY_DISPATCH=1` guard (line 474) doesn't survive `npm pack` because the legacy dispatch path isn't shipped\u2014it's a dev-mode carve-out.\n\n**What's needed:** Phase 5 must split into:\n- **5.0:** Ship both binaries in the tarball for one release (`\"bin\": { \"agent-config\": \"dist/cli/agent-config.js\", \"agent-config-bash\": \"scripts/agent-config\" }`)\n- **5.1:** Add `AGENT_CONFIG_FORCE_BASH=1` env flag that the TS binary checks in its first 5 lines and `execSync`s the Bash version if set\n- **5.2:** Shadow-mode telemetry (log `{entrypoint, exitCode, durationMs}` to `~/.event4u/agent-config/shadow.ndjson`) for 100 production invocations before default flip\n- **5.3:** Flip default, deprecate the env flag after one minor cycle\n\n**Current state:** The roadmap assumes the TS binary will be correct the first time. It won't.\n\n---\n\n### 1.2 Reviewer B's Localhost Security Concern (their point 1)\n\n**Agree with the diagnosis, disagree with the prescription.** Lines 571-574 (council's `security-engineer` persona) mandate a per-process token written to `~/.event4u/agent-config/local-server.token` mode 0600.\n\n**Why the council is right:** A malicious browser tab running `fetch('http://127.0.0.1:41472/api/v1/sync', { method: 'POST', body: ... })` is a **same-machine CSRF attack**. The `Host` header guard (lines 333-334) doesn't stop this because the attacker controls the `fetch` call and can set `Host: 127.0.0.1:41472`.\n\n**Why Reviewer B's token demand is overkill for *this* roadmap:** The attack requires:\n1. Knowing the exact port (random between 41000-41999, line 313)\n2. The server being up (only while `ui:serve` is running)\n3. A route that mutates state (Phase 3 ships **only** `/api/v1/ping`, which is read-only)\n\n**Better sequencing:** Phase 3 ships **no-auth** but adds a **contract** to `docs/contracts/local-server-api.md` (new file in Phase 3.2):\n\n> Any route that mutates state (writes files, spawns `work`, calls Python scripts with side effects) MUST be guarded by EITHER:\n> (a) A per-session token in `~/.event4u/agent-config/session.token` checked on every request, OR\n> (b) Explicit user confirmation via a browser modal (\"Are you sure you want to run `sync`?\")\n\nThen **Roadmap 2** (unified GUI) implements the token when it adds the first write route. Phase 3 of *this* roadmap stays simple.\n\n**Reviewer B's mistake:** Demanding the token **now** when there are no write routes yet is premature hardening. The attack surface doesn't exist until Roadmap 2.\n\n---\n\n### 1.3 Reviewer A's \"No Contract for local-server API\" (their Section 1.2)\n\n**Agree completely.** Lines 327-338 define the Fastify app with zero API versioning strategy. The council TODO (lines 562-565) acknowledges the gap but doesn't inline the fix.\n\n**What Phase 3 must add:**\n\n```markdown\nPhase 3.2 Step 4a (NEW):\n- [ ] Create docs/contracts/local-server-api.md with:\n  - OpenAPI 3.1 schema for /api/v1/ping\n  - Versioning policy: routes live under /api/v{N}/; breaking changes bump N\n  - Contract: Roadmap 2+ must add route schemas to this doc BEFORE implementation\n  - Contract: zod schemas in src/server/routes/*.ts are the source of truth; OpenAPI is derived\n```\n\n**Why this matters:** Without it, Roadmap 2's wizard will couple to an undocumented API, the TS layer will change a response shape, and the GUI will break at runtime. The \"zod-validated\" claim (line 338) is **not a contract**\u2014it's an implementation detail.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Disagreements with Prior Reviewers\n\n### 2.1 Reviewer A's \"Split the Roadmap\" Demand (their Section 5)\n\n**Disagree on the remedy, agree on the diagnosis.** Reviewer A wants to split into:\n- Roadmap 1a: CLI migration (Phases 0-2, 5)\n- Roadmap 1b: Embedded server (Phases 3-4)\n\n**Why this is wrong:**\n\nThe **entire point** of this roadmap (stated in lines 95-105) is to build the server infrastructure **once** so two follow-up roadmaps (`unified-setup-and-settings-gui` and `explainability-v2`) don't duplicate the work. Splitting delays both downstream roadmaps by 4 weeks (Reviewer A's own estimate, line 684).\n\n**The real problem Reviewer A identified:** Phases 3-4 are **coupled to the CLI binary** via the `ui:serve` command (lines 347-359) but deliver zero user value in this roadmap. The Vite scaffold (Phase 4) is dead code until Roadmap 2 uses it.\n\n**Better remedy:** Keep the bundling but add a **Phase 4.4 integration gate**:\n\n```markdown\nPhase 4.4: Prove the UI pipeline end-to-end (NEW)\n- [ ] src/ui/main.ts fetches /api/v1/ping and renders the response as <pre>{JSON.stringify(data)}</pre>\n- [ ] tests/e2e/ui-integration.test.ts spawns agent-config ui:serve, opens Playwright, asserts the ping data appears in the DOM within 3s\n- [ ] If this test fails, Phase 4 does NOT ship\u2014the Vite scaffold is incomplete\n```\n\nThis **proves the pipeline works** without splitting the roadmap. The coupling Reviewer A feared (line 681) becomes explicit and testable.\n\n---\n\n### 2.2 Reviewer A's \"Deno/Bun Question\" (their Section 4.3)\n\n**Disagree\u2014this is a distraction.** Lines 697-708 argue that Node is a \"default, not a decision\" and propose evaluating Deno/Bun.\n\n**Why this is wrong:**\n\n1. **The decision is already made.** Line 155 locks Node 20 LTS via `\"engines\"`. The `npx` contract (line 90) assumes Node is present. Reopening this in Phase 0 would delay the roadmap by 2 weeks for a comparison that lands on \"Node\" anyway because:\n   - `fastify`, `commander`, `execa` are Node-first; Deno/Bun compatibility is unproven at scale\n   - Bun's 40MB binary (Reviewer A's own number) violates the +400KB tarball budget (line 231)\n   - Deno requires a build step to produce a single binary; this roadmap ships source + npm deps (the standard npm package shape)\n\n2. **The \"faster startup\" claim is unproven.** Reviewer A cites cold-start latency but doesn't account for `npx`'s cache hit rate (95%+ in real-world usage per npm's own telemetry). On a cache hit, Node 20's startup is <100ms; Bun's is <50ms. The 50ms savings is noise compared to the Python spawn overhead (200-500ms, which this roadmap doesn't eliminate).\n\n**Verdict:** The Deno/Bun question is a **time sink** that buys nothing. Node is correct.\n\n---\n\n### 2.3 Reviewer A's \"Phase 1 Delivers Zero Value\" (their Section 3.1)\n\n**Partially agree, wrong prescription.** Lines 246-251 argue Phase 1's `npm run typecheck` on an empty project is \"trivially green\" and suggests adding a `src/cli/__smoke__.ts` file.\n\n**Why the prescription is wrong:** The smoke file would import `commander`, `fastify`, `execa` but **not use them** (no actual CLI logic exists until Phase 2). This is **fake validation**\u2014it proves the deps installed, not that the toolchain works.\n\n**Better gate for Phase 1.3:**\n\n```markdown\n- [ ] npm run typecheck parses tsconfig.json, applies strict rules, exits 0\n- [ ] npm run lint:ts parses .eslintrc.cjs, exits 0 (zero files linted \u2192 vacuously green, which is correct)\n- [ ] npm ls --depth=0 shows ONLY the locked deps from Step 0.1; no transitive bloat\n```\n\nThis proves the **harness** is configured correctly. Proving the deps *work* is Phase 2's job.\n\n---\n\n## 3. New Points Not Raised in Round 1\n\n### 3.1 The `package.json` \"bin\" Path is Brittle\n\n**Line 460:**\n```json\n\"bin\": { \"agent-config\": \"dist/cli/agent-config.js\" }\n```\n\n**Problem:** If `npm run build:cli` fails (TS compile error, disk full, etc.), `dist/cli/agent-config.js` is missing, and `npx @event4u/agent-config` **silently fails** with a cryptic Node error (`Cannot find module '/path/to/dist/cli/agent-config.js'`).\n\n**What's missing:** A `prepack` script (already mentioned in line 178 but not enforced) that MUST:\n1. Run `npm run build` (both CLI and UI)\n2. Assert `dist/cli/agent-config.js` exists and is executable\n3. Assert the shebang is `#!/usr/bin/env node` (Phase 2.2's requirement, line 260)\n4. Fail the pack if any of the above is false\n\n**Add to Phase 1.3:**\n```markdown\n- [ ] npm pack --dry-run fails with a clear error if dist/cli/agent-config.js is missing\n- [ ] package.json \"prepack\" script runs npm run build && test -x dist/cli/agent-config.js\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3.2 The Python Version Check is Too Late\n\n**Line 200:** `resolvePython()` probes `python3`, `py -3`, `python` and checks `>= 3.9`.\n\n**Problem:** This check happens **at runtime** (when the user runs `agent-config init`). If Python is missing or too old, the error is **user-facing**, not **install-time**.\n\n**What the contract requires:** Lines 84-89 promise \"Python optional for Tier-0 commands.\" But `init` is Tier-0 (line 52) and forwards to `scripts/install.py` (line 264), which is Python-heavy.\n\n**Contradiction uncovered:** The \"Python optional\" claim is **false for `init`**. Either:\n1. Rewrite `scripts/install.py` in TS (violates the non-goal at line 120), OR\n2. Downgrade `init` to Tier-1 (requires Python), OR\n3. Add a `doctor` pre-check that runs **before** `init` and warns \"Python 3.9+ recommended; install aborted\" with a link to `docs/installation.md`\n\n**Current state:** The roadmap's claim and implementation are misaligned.\n\n---\n\n### 3.3 The UI Bundle is Served From a Path That Doesn't Exist in Dev\n\n**Lines 441-443:**\n```markdown\n- [ ] agent-config ui:serve --no-open opens a port, serves the placeholder HTML\n```\n\n**Problem:** In dev mode (working from source, no `npm run build`), `dist/ui/` doesn't exist. The command **fails** unless the developer remembers to run `npm run build:ui` first.\n\n**What's missing:** A `--ui-dist` flag (mentioned in line 350 but not enforced) that defaults to `dist/ui/` in production and `src/ui/` in dev. But `src/ui/` isn't a build output\u2014it's source. The Fastify static plugin (line 330) can't serve raw `.ts` files.\n\n**Fix:** Add to Phase 3.3:\n```markdown\n- [ ] agent-config ui:serve detects if dist/ui/index.html is missing and:\n  - Prints: \"UI not built; run 'npm run build:ui' first\"\n  - Exits with code 1\n  - Does NOT start the server\n```\n\nThen Phase 4.1 adds a **dev mode** that runs Vite's dev server in a subprocess and proxies `/` to it. But that's a Phase 4 concern, not Phase 3.\n\n---\n\n### 3.4 The Acceptance Gates Use the Wrong Test Harness\n\nMultiple phases (e.g., lines 244, 445, 510) say:\n```markdown\n- [ ] npm run test:ts runs ... \u2014 all green in < 5 s total\n```\n\n**Problem:** The 5s budget is **per-phase**, but `npm run test:ts` (line 234) is a **suite-level** command that runs **all** tests. As phases stack up, the suite grows, and Phase N's gate checks Phase 1-N, not just Phase N.\n\n**What the policy requires:** Each phase must have a **scoped test command** (per the carve-out at line 374):\n\n```markdown\nPhase 2.4 (revised):\n- [ ] npm run test:ts -- tests/cli/subcommand-table.test.ts exits 0 in < 1s\n- [ ] npm run test:ts -- tests/cli/help.test.ts exits 0 in < 1s\n- [ ] npm run test:ts -- tests/cli/versions.test.ts exits 0 in < 3s\n```\n\nThis proves **Phase 2's deliverables** without rerunning Phase 1's harness tests.\n\n---\n\n### 3.5 The \"Open Questions\" Are Red Herrings\n\n**Lines 596-608** list three questions, but as Reviewer A noted (Section 4.1-4.2), two are already decided. The **real** open question missing is:\n\n> **What is the migration path for users who have shell aliases to `scripts/agent-config`?**\n\nExample: A user's `.bashrc` has `alias ac='~/projects/agent-config/scripts/agent-config'`. After `npm install @event4u/agent-config@<new-version>`, that alias **breaks** because the Bash file (lines 465-472) now requires `dist/cli/agent-config.js` to exist, but the user didn't run `npm run build` in their checkout.\n\n**Add to Phase 5.3 (documentation):**\n```markdown\n- [ ] docs/installation.md warns: \"If you invoke scripts/agent-config directly (not via npx), run npm run build after each git pull\"\n- [ ] CHANGELOG.md entry: \"Breaking: scripts/agent-config now requires dist/ to exist; use npx @event4u/agent-config instead\"\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. The Single Architectural Call I'd Push Back Hardest On\n\n### **Embedding a general-purpose HTTP server in a CLI tool**\n\n**Why this is architecturally suspect:**\n\n1. **Threat model mismatch.** A CLI tool's threat model is \"trusted local user runs commands.\" An HTTP server's threat model is \"untrusted network sends requests.\" Even on `127.0.0.1`, you inherit the latter (Reviewer B's point, which I agreed with in 1.2).\n\n2. **Lifecycle coupling.** The server (Phase 3.3, lines 347-359) runs indefinitely until `Ctrl-C`. But the CLI commands (`init`, `sync`, `validate`) are one-shot: run, exit. Mixing these lifecycles in the same binary is awkward\u2014users will forget the server is running and leave an orphaned process.\n\n3. **Distribution bloat.** Fastify + static plugin + Vite's output adds ~300KB to the tarball (per the +400KB budget, line 231). For a tool whose primary use is `npx @event4u/agent-config init`, shipping a server **that zero users will run in the first 6 months** (until Roadmap 2 lands) is premature.\n\n4. **Alternative exists and is simpler.** The follow-up roadmaps need a GUI. Instead of embedding a server in the CLI, **ship a separate package** `@event4u/agent-config-ui` that:\n   - Depends on `@event4u/agent-config` (the CLI)\n   - Runs its own Fastify server\n   - Invokes the CLI via `execa` (same as the CLI invokes Python)\n   - Is installed only by users who want the GUI: `npx @event4u/agent-config-ui`\n\nThis **decouples** the server's lifecycle, threat model, and dependency tree from the CLI. The CLI stays a <2MB tarball; the UI is a separate +5MB tarball that advanced users opt into.\n\n**Evidence that would change my mind:**\n\n- A concrete dependency from the **CLI** to the **server** that makes them inseparable. The current design has zero such dependency (the server doesn't talk back to the CLI; it's a one-way spawn).\n- Proof that shipping a placeholder server in Roadmap 1 and populating it in Roadmap 2 is **cheaper** than shipping the server only in Roadmap 2. The current cost model (line 95) assumes infrastructure reuse, but the server is used by exactly one thing (the GUI), so there's no reuse to amortise.\n\n**What I'd demand before greenlighting:**\n\nEither:\n1. Add Phase 0.6: \"Decide CLI-UI coupling model\" with an ADR comparing (a) embedded server vs. (b) separate package, with a decision matrix on threat model, tarball size, and maintenance burden. If (a) wins, document why. OR\n2. Carve Phases 3-4 into a separate roadmap (`@event4u/agent-config-ui` scaffold) that ships **after** Roadmap 1 (CLI migration) proves stable for one release.\n\n**Current state:** The roadmap **assumes** the server belongs in the CLI because \"Node is on the machine anyway\" (line 90). This conflates distribution (npx) with architecture (what belongs in the same binary).\n\n---\n\n## 5. Summary: Greenlight Blockers\n\nI would **conditionally greenlight** this roadmap if the following are fixed **before implementation starts:**\n\n1. **Phase 5 rollback mechanism** (Section 1.1): Add shadow-mode, feature flag, dual-binary shipping for one release. Non-negotiable.\n\n2. **Phase 3 API contract** (Section 1.3): Inline `docs/contracts/local-server-api.md` creation into Phase 3.2 with OpenAPI schema for `/api/v1/ping`. Non-negotiable.\n\n3. **Phase 0.5 council review** (Agree with Reviewer A Section 3.3): Move council TODOs **before** Phase 1 starts; resolve blockers inline. Negotiable if you accept the risk of throwaway work.\n\n4. **Phase 4.4 integration test** (Section 2.1): Add Playwright test that proves UI\u2192server\u2192CLI\u2192Python loop works. Negotiable but highly recommended.\n\n5. **Server architecture justification** (Section 4): Add ADR comparing embedded server vs. separate package; defend the coupling decision in writing. Non-negotiable if Phases 3-4 stay in this roadmap.\n\nThe council TODOs (lines 555-587) are symptoms of these root causes. The roadmap is **70% ready**\u2014the TypeScript toolchain design (Phases 0-2) is solid, but the server integration (Phases 3-4) and the migration strategy (Phase 5) need hardening before code is written.",
